+# Affine isomorphisms of arc structures (arXiv:2302.13657)
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate | exact symbolic affine map `Phi(x)=u*x+b` |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: a field-valued step-set statistic scales with the hidden multiplier |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## What the problem is
+
+[Dalmau, Krokhin, and Opršal, *Functors on relational structures which admit both left and right adjoints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13657) defines homomorphisms of relational structures in Definition 2.2, central Pultr functors in Definition 2.8, and the three-relation arc structure in Section 6.2. Its vertices are directed arcs; relations `D`, `I`, and `O` record consecutive arcs, a common head, and a common tail. Section 6 observes that a digraph homomorphism induces a homomorphism on the functor images.
+
+An instance gives two cyclic Cayley digraphs over the same prime field by their exact step sets `S` and `T`. The solver must give the two coefficients of an affine vertex map. That map induces an isomorphism of the two arc structures. Verification is cheap: check the coefficient ranges and the exact set identity `u*S = T (mod p)`; the three incidence equalities are then preserved symbolically. The generator samples `u` first and builds `T=u*S`, so it never solves its own instance.
+
+## Why Track B is honest
+
+This paper contains no distributional hardness theorem for planted homomorphism instances, so Track A would be unsupported. The restricted certificate language here also has an efficient exact algorithm: choose one source step, try each target step as its image, and compare the resulting complete step set. This anchor-image algorithm is `O(m^2)` and succeeds on every instance. At the provisional hard preset its eight-seed median is 14,312 field operations and 0.00097 seconds.
+
+The intended compression is the covariant sum. The source construction has nonzero field sum, so `sum(T)=u*sum(S)` uniquely determines `u`; any `b` works. This takes 280 exact field operations at hard rather than candidate-map enumeration. The paper result that makes the certificate construction easy is the functorial induced-map statement preceding Definition 6.1 and its arc-structure specialization in Section 6.2—not an NP-hardness result. The near-symmetric subgroup construction makes many wrong multipliers look locally plausible while retaining that short invariant route.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=11, step_count=5, seed=0)`, the rendered data are:
+
+```text
+p = 11
+S = [1, 2, 6, 7, 8]
+T = [2, 5, 7, 8, 10]
+Phi(x,s) = (u*x+b mod 11, u*s mod 11)
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>5, 0</answer>`. Here `5*S mod 11 = T`, so `verify(inst, [5,0])` returns `(True, "ok")`. The corruption `[1,0]` returns `(False, "the affine map sends steps to the wrong set (2 missing, 2 extra)")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by testing the ten nonzero multipliers or by observing that `sum(S)=2`, `sum(T)=10`, and hence `u=5` modulo 11.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | requested `n` | actual prime `p` | steps `m` | intended operations | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 11 | 11 | 5 | 10 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 2,000,000 | 2,000,221 | 60 | 120 | awaiting oracle ladder |
+| medium | 3,000,000 | 3,000,301 | 100 | 200 | awaiting oracle ladder |
+| hard | 5,000,000 | 5,000,101 | 140 | 280 | provisional shipping preset |
+
+Difficulty grows by enlarging the prime field and the crowd of near-symmetric steps while the answer remains two integers. `escalate()` continues on those axes at fixed answer length.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted certificates verify; 12/12 JSON round-trips |
+| G2 | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range corruptions all rejected with distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged answer recovered from prose and a Markdown fence; garbage rejected |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; exact probability `1/(p-1) = 1.99996e-7` at hard |
+| G5 | exactly `p=5,000,101` valid affine pairs; reference median 14,312 operations / 0.00097 s |
+| G6 | five attacks, 0/8 successes each; reference and compact algorithms both solve 8/8 as expected |
+| G7 | doubled request builds at prime 10,001,281, verifies, and has a larger search space with two answer atoms |
+| G8 | 80/80 affine/reordering invariance checks and 80/80 carried witnesses; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | local caps pass (9 characters, 3 estimated tokens, 2 atoms, 280 operations); oracle-backed arms pending |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The first required harness attempt was not scoreable: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded` on every retry. These errors are retained in `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` and are not counted as model failures. A valid shipping verdict and the hinted/placebo arms still require rerunning `scripts/harden.py` after the workspace key limit is restored.
+
+| run | preset | solved/attempts | result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 scoreable | blocked by four HTTP 403 errors |
+| structural hint | — | 0/0 | pending |
+| placebo hint | — | 0/0 | pending |
+
+Hinted minus placebo is not yet measurable. The answer-size measurement is 9 characters, about 3 tokens, and 2 atomic elements; the intended hard route is 280 exact field operations.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2302_13657 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=0, **g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY])
+print(g.render(inst))
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>123, 0</answer>")
+print(g.verify(inst, candidate))
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit instances with `bash scripts/emit.sh 2302.13657 20` after the oracle evidence is complete.
+
+## Caveats
+
+This benchmark asks for an affine induced map, not an arbitrary homomorphism, and therefore makes no NP-hardness claim. With a sandbox, even the reference algorithm finishes in milliseconds; this is exactly why the family is Track B. The guess probability is uniform over the bounded affine language after enforcing coefficient ranges; it says nothing about a solver prior that has discovered the sum invariant. The cheap panel tests minimum-residue alignment, sorted greedy alignment, an additive ansatz, small multipliers, and random restarts. It does not test general graph-isomorphism software, Weisfeiler–Leman refinement on the exponentially expanded presentation, or a multiplicative-energy analysis of the near-subgroup step sets; tool-assisted algebraic methods are expected to succeed. Finally, the two Cayley/arc structures are given succinctly rather than by listing their millions of vertices and relations, but their definitions and exact verifier are the paper's native finite relational objects, not a surrogate graph reduction.
