/tmp/arxiv2302.13657.t6eoSC/src/arxiv.tex:121:  We will write $\rel A \to \rel B$ if there exists a homomorphism from $\rel A$ to $\rel B$. The set of all homomorphisms from $\rel A$ to $\rel B$ is denoted by $\hom(\rel A, \rel B)$.
/tmp/arxiv2302.13657.t6eoSC/src/arxiv.tex:130:Finally, since we will be extensively working with the homomorphism preorder, this in particular means that we will often work with structures up to homomorphic equivalence --- we say that two structures $\rel A$ and $\rel B$ are \emph{homomorphically equivalent} if we have $\rel A \to \rel B$ and $\rel B \to \rel A$.  Such structures would be identified if we followed the standard procedure to turn the homomorphism preorder into a proper partial order.
