+## Problem and trust model
 
-[Dalmau, Krokhin, and Opršal, *Functors on relational structures which admit both left and right adjoints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13657) defines homomorphisms of relational structures in Definition 2.2, central Pultr functors in Definition 2.8, and the three-relation arc structure in Section 6.2. Its vertices are directed arcs; relations `D`, `I`, and `O` record consecutive arcs, a common head, and a common tail. Section 6 observes that a digraph homomorphism induces a homomorphism on the functor images.
+[Dalmau, Krokhin, and Opršal, *Functors on relational structures which admit both left and right adjoints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13657) defines relation-preserving maps in Definition 2.2 and the three-relation arc structure in Section 6.2. Its vertices are directed arcs; `D`, `I`, and `O` record consecutive arcs, a common head, and a common tail. Section 6 also states that a digraph homomorphism induces a homomorphism between the corresponding functor images.
 
-An instance gives two cyclic Cayley digraphs over the same prime field by their exact step sets `S` and `T`. The solver must give the two coefficients of an affine vertex map. That map induces an isomorphism of the two arc structures. Verification is cheap: check the coefficient ranges and the exact set identity `u*S = T (mod p)`; the three incidence equalities are then preserved symbolically. The generator samples `u` first and builds `T=u*S`, so it never solves its own instance.
