+## Problem and trust model
+
+[Dalmau, Krokhin, and Opršal, *Functors on relational structures which admit both left and right adjoints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.13657) defines relation-preserving maps in Definition 2.2 and the three-relation arc structure in Section 6.2. Its vertices are directed arcs; `D`, `I`, and `O` record consecutive arcs, a common head, and a common tail. Section 6 also states that a digraph homomorphism induces a homomorphism between the corresponding functor images.
+
+An instance gives two cyclic Cayley digraphs over the same prime field by exact step sets `S` and `T`. The answer is the two coefficients of an affine vertex map, which induces a bijective homomorphism of their arc structures. Verification checks coefficient ranges and the exact identity `u*S = T (mod p)`; the three incidence relations are then preserved symbolically. Generation is inverse: sample `u`, form `T=u*S`, and retain `[u,0]`. No instance is solved during generation.
+
+This is honestly Track B. The paper gives no distributional hardness theorem for planted homomorphisms—in fact its conclusion says no specific promise-CSP hardness application is known beyond the arc-graph use it cites—so Track A would be unsupported. The exact reference method enumerates the image of one source step and checks each possible multiplier: `O(m^2)`, with an eight-seed hard-preset median of 5,385 counted field operations and 0.0027 seconds in the final self-test. The compact route notices covariance of the set sum. The source set is constructed with sum one, so after summing both displayed sets the target sum is `u`; this is 238 modular additions at hard. The paper-side easy mechanism is functoriality itself (Section 6, before Definition 6.1), not a hardness theorem.
 
